00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:11.850
Anyone can build a model.

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:15.010
And we see so many of those
come up on GitHub every day.

00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:17.670
But when we are talking
about an end-to-end solution,

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:20.112
it involves more than
just the model itself.

00:01:20.112 --> 00:01:21.570
MARTIN OMANDER:
What amount of work

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:23.640
do you think you put it
into this sort of data

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:26.190
engineering versus building the
model, do you think, Vikram?

00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:28.232
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah,
actually, building the model

00:01:28.232 --> 00:01:29.160
itself is really easy.

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:31.590
Because it's-- most of the time,
you are just taking a paper

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:33.965
that has recently published,
because you wantto always be

00:01:33.965 --> 00:01:34.470
updated.

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:36.730
And then you cut it down.

00:01:36.730 --> 00:01:38.700
You take the mathematics
that is in the model

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:40.830
to transform it to your
Python function-- or maybe

00:01:40.830 --> 00:01:42.750
if you are using Swift
or something else.

00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:45.300
And convert that model--
converting that model code is

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:46.710
not actually really hard.

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:48.660
It's mostly like
getting your data

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:51.295
to work with that model,
which takes more time.

00:01:51.295 --> 00:01:52.170
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah.

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:53.040
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah,
as we are talking--

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:55.000
going to be talking
about here today,

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:57.030
when you are building
this complete product,

00:01:57.030 --> 00:01:58.760
it takes a lot more to do that.

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:00.820
Because you have to extract
and clean your data.

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:03.240
You have to do that
over time in a pipeline.

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:04.020
You have to do--

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:05.862
and apply extra data
transformations.

00:02:05.862 --> 00:02:07.320
Because those are
your new features

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:08.610
that you have to apply.

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:10.449
Then you have to
manage this whole model

00:02:10.449 --> 00:02:11.730
pipeline that is there.

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:13.770
So the configuration
management of that,

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:15.540
you're doing using
hyperparameter

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:17.490
tuning and similar things.

00:02:17.490 --> 00:02:20.280
And then, your models
are not good enough

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:22.650
unless they can actually
give value to the user.

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:25.710
So you have to provide on-demand
predictions to the users.

00:02:25.710 --> 00:02:27.090
MARTIN OMANDER: Right, and
this never stops, right?

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:28.350
VIKRAM TIWARI: This never stops.

00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:30.480
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah, you don't
build a model, and you're done.

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:31.313
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah.

00:02:31.313 --> 00:02:34.445
So a product is like-- it's not
a one-time proof of concept.

00:02:34.445 --> 00:02:36.820
You have to build the model
and keep the machine running.

00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:37.950
And there's new data coming in.

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:40.200
There's new machine learning
models getting out there.

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:43.810
And you have to constantly
give the value to the client.

00:02:43.810 --> 00:02:45.930
So for that, this
is a whole pipeline.

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:47.310
This is not a one-stop--

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:49.857
one-way-- or one process.

00:02:49.857 --> 00:02:52.440
So there are a few engineering
challenges that we had to face.

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:53.857
We categorized it
into four parts.

00:02:53.857 --> 00:02:55.530
And that's how we'll
be walking today,

00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:57.430
through the presentation.

00:02:57.430 --> 00:03:00.180
So first is extracting and
transforming your data.

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:03.240
So extracting the data--
we are doing this via APIs

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:04.250
from these vendors.

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:07.780
These APIs have limitations
that you cannot extract data all

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:08.280
the time.

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:10.840
It's not a socket-based API.

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:13.470
Or every vendor will have
different restrictions.

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:15.323
You have to have
quotas on those APIs.

00:03:15.323 --> 00:03:16.740
And we have to
honor those quotas.

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:18.065
Otherwise, we will get blocked.

00:03:18.065 --> 00:03:19.740
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, it
sounds like that might

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:20.823
have happened in the past.

00:03:20.823 --> 00:03:22.770
VIKRAM TIWARI:
Yeah, you know it.

00:03:22.770 --> 00:03:26.340
So then, all the data, that
raw data that you get it,

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:28.225
you have to also
transform that data.

00:03:28.225 --> 00:03:30.600
So you have to transform the
data for multiple use cases.

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:32.700
Because analytics
is, today's use case.

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:33.480
What's next?

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:35.550
Machine learning, of course.

00:03:35.550 --> 00:03:37.560
Then, once you have
all of this data,

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:39.930
you want to store that
in a central location

00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:43.350
where you can use it for
reporting as well as analytics

00:03:43.350 --> 00:03:44.700
use cases.

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:49.870
And then you can store it all
for use cases of analytics,

00:03:49.870 --> 00:03:51.862
but your queries
might become slow.

00:03:51.862 --> 00:03:54.070
So you have to make sure
that your queries are always

00:03:54.070 --> 00:03:55.000
performant.

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:56.980
And they should always
be returning the results

00:03:56.980 --> 00:03:58.600
in a performant way.

00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:00.310
Users don't want slow queries.

00:04:00.310 --> 00:04:02.290
Nobody loves that.

00:04:02.290 --> 00:04:03.920
And keep the cost low as well.

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:05.420
So you want to scale
up the servers.

00:04:05.420 --> 00:04:06.573
We'll talk more about that.

00:04:06.573 --> 00:04:08.740
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah, Omni
Labs is a startup, right?

00:04:08.740 --> 00:04:09.060
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah.

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:11.795
MARTIN OMANDER: So you're
looking at costs quite a bit.

00:04:11.795 --> 00:04:13.045
VIKRAM TIWARI: Almost all day.

00:04:13.045 --> 00:04:15.253
MARTIN OMANDER: Right, and
we'll see more about that,

00:04:15.253 --> 00:04:17.980
and how to manage costs,
and make this all efficient.

00:04:17.980 --> 00:04:19.480
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah.

00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:21.430
Then, once you
have all the data,

00:04:21.430 --> 00:04:24.520
you have to actually build your
models and keep them updated.

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:26.140
Keeping the model
updated is not just

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:27.490
keeping the model code updated.

00:04:27.490 --> 00:04:29.740
Of course, you have to update
your Python dependencies

00:04:29.740 --> 00:04:30.340
all the time.

00:04:30.340 --> 00:04:33.460
But you have to also make sure
that it's getting the new data.

00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:35.930
There is always--
our advertisers,

00:04:35.930 --> 00:04:38.270
they're actually running
the campaigns every day.

00:04:38.270 --> 00:04:41.260
So you want to get all that data
every day, or as much frequency

00:04:41.260 --> 00:04:43.840
as you can, and generate
the newer model.

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:45.730
Because your model is
as good as the data

00:04:45.730 --> 00:04:47.720
that is getting in there.

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:49.888
Then, you also have
to manage retraining,

00:04:49.888 --> 00:04:51.930
because there are new
clients that are coming in.

00:04:51.930 --> 00:04:54.500
You want to use one central
model for all the clients.

00:04:54.500 --> 00:04:57.140
Or you want to generate new
models out of those models--

00:04:57.140 --> 00:04:59.000
the central model that you have.

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:00.660
And of course,
hyperparameter tuning,

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:01.970
who doesn't want to do that?

00:05:01.970 --> 00:05:03.992
You never can build a
good model by yourself.

00:05:03.992 --> 00:05:06.200
You have to use hyperparameter
tuning to get the best

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:07.910
hyperparameters.

00:05:07.910 --> 00:05:10.010
And finally, all
of this is no value

00:05:10.010 --> 00:05:13.010
unless you can serve this, in
real time, to your clients.

00:05:13.010 --> 00:05:15.020
So managing authentication--
which client

00:05:15.020 --> 00:05:19.310
gets access to which model that
you have in-house, depending

00:05:19.310 --> 00:05:21.890
upon how much they're
paying, and all of this

00:05:21.890 --> 00:05:24.630
managing in real-time
predictions.

00:05:24.630 --> 00:05:26.300
And this traffic
is very infrequent,

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:28.220
as we will talk
more in the talk--

00:05:28.220 --> 00:05:30.610
more in the presentation today.

00:05:30.610 --> 00:05:31.740
MARTIN OMANDER: Excellent.

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:36.610
So with this, a lot of this
can be done in serverless.

00:05:36.610 --> 00:05:39.840
You have all heard-- we've
all heard about serverless.

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:42.900
And serverless
computing-- there's

00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:44.760
one part of serverless
that gets most

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:49.090
of the press, most of the blog
posts, most of the attention.

00:05:49.090 --> 00:05:51.360
And that is the central
part of the figure

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:54.380
here, the compute part.

00:05:54.380 --> 00:05:57.550
So serverless computing,
just as a quick recap--

00:05:57.550 --> 00:06:02.520
in the Google Cloud Platform,
you could do Cloud Functions.

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:05.040
If you have just very simple
pieces of code functions,

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:06.720
you can string them together.

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:08.710
Or if you want to
deploy something bigger,

00:06:08.710 --> 00:06:13.830
like a full application, you
would run those on App Engine.

00:06:13.830 --> 00:06:15.990
Yesterday, you may have
heard the announcement

00:06:15.990 --> 00:06:17.340
about Cloud Run.

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:20.690
That is the third hexagon
we see there in the middle.

00:06:20.690 --> 00:06:25.800
This allows you to upload,
deploy a container,

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:27.870
and run that serverlessly.

00:06:27.870 --> 00:06:28.920
So serverless compute--

00:06:28.920 --> 00:06:30.810
OK, we kind of
know what that is.

00:06:30.810 --> 00:06:32.550
But there's so much else--

00:06:32.550 --> 00:06:35.590
so much more-- to serverless.

00:06:35.590 --> 00:06:39.970
Because we, as developers, want
to solve business problems, not

00:06:39.970 --> 00:06:42.220
engineering problems.

00:06:42.220 --> 00:06:46.420
So that becomes a lot
easier if we do computations

00:06:46.420 --> 00:06:51.010
serverlessly, but also if we
do data analytics, database

00:06:51.010 --> 00:06:54.760
storage, ML, devops, messaging.

00:06:54.760 --> 00:06:59.180
All these other things can
be done serverlessly as well.

00:06:59.180 --> 00:07:04.195
And we'll see some cases here
where Vikram will tell us

00:07:04.195 --> 00:07:06.910
how his startup, Omni
Labs, achieved greater dev

00:07:06.910 --> 00:07:11.020
velocity by going serverless
on some of these other pieces

00:07:11.020 --> 00:07:13.090
as well, not just Compute.

00:07:16.300 --> 00:07:17.800
So first, we have--

00:07:17.800 --> 00:07:21.100
Vikram was mentioning--
extracting and transforming

00:07:21.100 --> 00:07:22.550
the data.

00:07:22.550 --> 00:07:25.220
So these data
pipelines, as we heard,

00:07:25.220 --> 00:07:27.110
they extract the
data from the APIs.

00:07:27.110 --> 00:07:29.830
They clean it, merge
it with other data.

00:07:29.830 --> 00:07:32.830
And we have some
engineering challenges here.

00:07:32.830 --> 00:07:35.233
It's not just a simple
FTP import or something,

00:07:35.233 --> 00:07:35.900
and you're done.

00:07:35.900 --> 00:07:36.790
VIKRAM TIWARI: GPN portal, man.

00:07:36.790 --> 00:07:37.660
MARTIN OMANDER: Yep.

00:07:37.660 --> 00:07:39.370
VIKRAM TIWARI: So we are
getting all this data

00:07:39.370 --> 00:07:40.330
from different APIs.

00:07:40.330 --> 00:07:41.177
We have to clean it.

00:07:41.177 --> 00:07:42.760
Of course, there's
feature engineering

00:07:42.760 --> 00:07:44.030
that you have to do.

00:07:44.030 --> 00:07:46.660
And the biggest challenges
that we faced on our side

00:07:46.660 --> 00:07:48.238
was the variability
of the data size.

00:07:48.238 --> 00:07:49.780
Because there could
be a client which

00:07:49.780 --> 00:07:51.400
is running a very
small campaign,

00:07:51.400 --> 00:07:52.948
so they are getting
10 entries a day,

00:07:52.948 --> 00:07:54.490
versus there could
be a client, which

00:07:54.490 --> 00:07:56.770
is running thousands of
campaign across geographies,

00:07:56.770 --> 00:07:59.110
and we are getting thousands
of entries per hour.

00:07:59.110 --> 00:08:02.010
So data size varies
from client to client.

00:08:02.010 --> 00:08:04.240
Plus, frequency of data
ingestion could also vary.

00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:06.520
There could be somebody who is
not running any campaigns right

00:08:06.520 --> 00:08:07.030
now.

00:08:07.030 --> 00:08:09.823
We don't want to keep machines
up for those ingestions.

00:08:09.823 --> 00:08:11.740
Or there could be somebody
who is running this

00:08:11.740 --> 00:08:13.690
and wants this data
every few hours.

00:08:13.690 --> 00:08:16.270
And we want to make sure that
our solution can deliver this

00:08:16.270 --> 00:08:17.080
to them.

00:08:17.080 --> 00:08:18.580
MARTIN OMANDER: So
you couldn't have

00:08:18.580 --> 00:08:20.080
a sort of one size fits all.

00:08:20.080 --> 00:08:20.330
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah.

00:08:20.330 --> 00:08:22.110
MARTIN OMANDER: Is that
what made this hard?

00:08:22.110 --> 00:08:23.152
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah, so--

00:08:23.152 --> 00:08:25.828
yeah, so what we tried
was, we tried to--

00:08:25.828 --> 00:08:28.120
as soon as you have a data
problem, first thing you do,

00:08:28.120 --> 00:08:30.460
you write up Python code, put
it in our virtual machine.

00:08:30.460 --> 00:08:34.480
So we tried that, but there
were multiple issues there.

00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:37.730
What could happen is we could
allocate access compute.

00:08:37.730 --> 00:08:39.100
So if there was--

00:08:39.100 --> 00:08:40.929
data size was
really small and we

00:08:40.929 --> 00:08:45.320
had given it a 10-CPU machine,
eh, not really required.

00:08:45.320 --> 00:08:48.490
Sometimes it might be
a really big data set,

00:08:48.490 --> 00:08:51.550
and we didn't look at enough
CPU or enough RAM in there.

00:08:51.550 --> 00:08:55.800
And RAM is not compressable, so
it crashes the whole machine.

00:08:55.800 --> 00:08:58.270
That also happens.

00:08:58.270 --> 00:09:01.170
Sometimes this whole
process, since it's

00:09:01.170 --> 00:09:03.667
happening in a chain
process, joint chain manner--

00:09:03.667 --> 00:09:05.250
first, we get the
data on one machine.

00:09:05.250 --> 00:09:07.000
Then we transform it
on another machine.

00:09:07.000 --> 00:09:08.833
Then we pass it forward
to the third machine

00:09:08.833 --> 00:09:11.077
to push it into different
storage sections--

00:09:11.077 --> 00:09:12.660
this whole process
is chained together

00:09:12.660 --> 00:09:14.010
with different scripts.

00:09:14.010 --> 00:09:16.840
And if one of those breaks,
the whole system collapses.

00:09:16.840 --> 00:09:17.760
It's very brittle.

00:09:17.760 --> 00:09:22.180
And the collapses-- any breaking
causes cascading collapses.

00:09:22.180 --> 00:09:26.310
MARTIN OMANDER: Hmm, so this,
is of course, managed VMs,

00:09:26.310 --> 00:09:28.020
as we heard.

00:09:28.020 --> 00:09:30.840
There is another way to,
potentially, doing this.

00:09:30.840 --> 00:09:35.730
With Cloud Dataflow, you
don't manage servers.

00:09:35.730 --> 00:09:38.610
It can take batch
or streams of data

00:09:38.610 --> 00:09:42.240
and then serverlessly,
dynamically

00:09:42.240 --> 00:09:44.290
allocate these jobs--

00:09:44.290 --> 00:09:46.510
so across physical machines.

00:09:46.510 --> 00:09:48.450
And you don't really see
the physical machines

00:09:48.450 --> 00:09:50.310
or manage them much.

00:09:50.310 --> 00:09:53.020
Would that be something that
would be useful for Omni Labs?

00:09:53.020 --> 00:09:55.020
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah, so
that's why, actually, we

00:09:55.020 --> 00:09:55.980
ended up doing.

00:09:55.980 --> 00:09:58.710
So we ended up
utilizing Dataflow.

00:09:58.710 --> 00:10:00.780
And with Dataflow, what
we were able to do is

00:10:00.780 --> 00:10:04.360
we were able to launch as many
jobs as we can in parallel.

00:10:04.360 --> 00:10:06.448
So we split off our jobs
into smaller chunks.

00:10:06.448 --> 00:10:08.490
As you can see from the
image, we launched, like,

00:10:08.490 --> 00:10:10.080
10 jobs within a minute.

00:10:10.080 --> 00:10:12.430
And all of those 10 jobs
are running in parallel.

00:10:12.430 --> 00:10:14.330
And if one of those
jobs fails, it

00:10:14.330 --> 00:10:17.160
doesn't cause any issues with
another job that is running.

00:10:17.160 --> 00:10:19.110
So other systems
can keep running,

00:10:19.110 --> 00:10:21.890
and one individual
job fails by itself.

00:10:21.890 --> 00:10:24.660
Also, all the machines,
since they are--

00:10:24.660 --> 00:10:26.460
they're scalable by themselves.

00:10:26.460 --> 00:10:28.940
So if your job runs in
seven minutes or seven days,

00:10:28.940 --> 00:10:30.410
your machines can keep alive.

00:10:30.410 --> 00:10:32.970
System scheduler will
take care of that.

00:10:32.970 --> 00:10:35.360
And they can scale up and
down as your data flow

00:10:35.360 --> 00:10:36.800
transformations are happening.

00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:38.630
MARTIN OMANDER: You had jobs
that ran for seven days?

00:10:38.630 --> 00:10:40.850
VIKRAM TIWARI: A few, and
some for more than that.

00:10:40.850 --> 00:10:41.900
MARTIN OMANDER: More than that?

00:10:41.900 --> 00:10:43.290
VIKRAM TIWARI: I didn't have
the screenshots for those.

00:10:43.290 --> 00:10:44.800
MARTIN OMANDER: Wow,
that's a big job.

00:10:44.800 --> 00:10:45.420
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah.

00:10:45.420 --> 00:10:47.630
Yeah, so we were doing most
of these as batch jobs.

00:10:47.630 --> 00:10:50.540
We have started doing most of
these, now, as streaming jobs.

00:10:50.540 --> 00:10:52.258
So it's always running machines.

00:10:52.258 --> 00:10:54.425
There are some people who
have done these jobs where

00:10:54.425 --> 00:10:56.190
the machines have been
running for years now.

00:10:56.190 --> 00:10:57.050
MARTIN OMANDER: Wow.

00:10:57.050 --> 00:10:58.130
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah.

00:10:58.130 --> 00:11:03.620
So while doing this, we came
into touch with Apache Beam.

00:11:03.620 --> 00:11:05.600
Apache Beam is the
programming model

00:11:05.600 --> 00:11:09.110
that you use to
transform your data

00:11:09.110 --> 00:11:11.240
and run this on a
Dataflow pipeline.

00:11:11.240 --> 00:11:14.160
Apache Beam provides you
an out-of-box methods.

00:11:14.160 --> 00:11:18.730
So there's reading from a file,
or splitting your data into--

00:11:18.730 --> 00:11:20.630
or creating a flatMap.

00:11:20.630 --> 00:11:23.090
Plus, you can create
your own functions

00:11:23.090 --> 00:11:25.640
and apply those into the--

00:11:25.640 --> 00:11:27.870
utilize those custom
functions as well.

00:11:27.870 --> 00:11:31.190
The best thing about Apache
Beam is your pipeline options,

00:11:31.190 --> 00:11:33.010
as you can see on the top.

00:11:33.010 --> 00:11:35.160
If you want to take
it away from GCP,

00:11:35.160 --> 00:11:36.640
you just change
pipeline options,

00:11:36.640 --> 00:11:39.520
and you are on your local
machines, another cloud

00:11:39.520 --> 00:11:41.927
provider, or anywhere
else you want to run it.

00:11:41.927 --> 00:11:43.510
And it's just change
of configuration.

00:11:43.510 --> 00:11:44.510
Your code never changes.

00:11:44.510 --> 00:11:46.052
MARTIN OMANDER: You
would, of course,

00:11:46.052 --> 00:11:47.500
never want to take
it off of GCP.

00:11:47.500 --> 00:11:49.967
But just in case, it's nice
to have an escape hatch.

00:11:49.967 --> 00:11:50.800
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah.

00:11:53.920 --> 00:11:57.130
MARTIN OMANDER:
All right, so yeah,

00:11:57.130 --> 00:11:59.040
you should never be a prisoner--

00:11:59.040 --> 00:12:02.560
if your cloud platform,
whatever vendor you're using,

00:12:02.560 --> 00:12:05.680
is keeping your data and
code prisoner on there,

00:12:05.680 --> 00:12:07.840
then it's probably
not a place to be.

00:12:07.840 --> 00:12:10.030
You want to always be able
to have the escape hatch.

00:12:10.030 --> 00:12:12.730
It's sort of like, if
you're on a plane, right,

00:12:12.730 --> 00:12:17.950
you probably don't want to use
the emergency exits too often.

00:12:17.950 --> 00:12:21.650
But it's good to know
that they're there.

00:12:21.650 --> 00:12:24.220
So the next step Vikram
was telling us about

00:12:24.220 --> 00:12:27.430
is filling the data lake.

00:12:27.430 --> 00:12:30.580
So data lake is
this pattern where

00:12:30.580 --> 00:12:34.000
you getting the data
from various resources

00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:37.180
like these APIs, external APIs.

00:12:37.180 --> 00:12:41.500
And instead of crunching
it as you are importing it,

00:12:41.500 --> 00:12:45.730
you just store it
all in raw form.

00:12:45.730 --> 00:12:47.140
Then, when you
need to-- when you

00:12:47.140 --> 00:12:50.110
have certain data needs, or
reports, or machine learning,

00:12:50.110 --> 00:12:53.180
that's when you
apply the transforms.

00:12:53.180 --> 00:12:55.520
That means that you
can do things like--

00:12:55.520 --> 00:13:00.230
I want to have everything by
week, and grouped by week.

00:13:00.230 --> 00:13:04.040
Or the next month, you want
everything grouped by hour.

00:13:04.040 --> 00:13:07.100
And you can get that, because
you have all the raw data.

00:13:09.620 --> 00:13:16.070
So the data lake is the central
source for all your raw data.

00:13:16.070 --> 00:13:20.150
And you saw some real challenges
here with filling the lake.

00:13:20.150 --> 00:13:21.030
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah.

00:13:21.030 --> 00:13:23.330
Yeah, as I mentioned
already, there

00:13:23.330 --> 00:13:26.190
could be thousands of entries
which could come in every hour.

00:13:26.190 --> 00:13:28.580
So what you want is a
really good ingestion system

00:13:28.580 --> 00:13:30.080
which is write-heavy.

00:13:30.080 --> 00:13:32.210
And then you also
want to make sure

00:13:32.210 --> 00:13:34.705
that your analytics can
aggregate really quickly.

00:13:34.705 --> 00:13:36.080
Because you are
going to be doing

00:13:36.080 --> 00:13:39.050
a lot of operations on different
times and different window

00:13:39.050 --> 00:13:40.250
options.

00:13:40.250 --> 00:13:42.860
Plus, you also want to
be able to do modeling

00:13:42.860 --> 00:13:44.090
on top of this data.

00:13:44.090 --> 00:13:46.310
So you will have
different-- window sessions

00:13:46.310 --> 00:13:47.610
could be really huge.

00:13:47.610 --> 00:13:49.402
It could be years of
data that you could be

00:13:49.402 --> 00:13:51.610
using to do the modeling patch.

00:13:51.610 --> 00:13:55.230
So the initial approach that
we went with was dump and pray.

00:13:55.230 --> 00:13:57.290
So we took all the data
that we were getting in

00:13:57.290 --> 00:13:59.240
through our pipelines
and pushed all of that

00:13:59.240 --> 00:14:01.040
into BigQuery straight up.

00:14:01.040 --> 00:14:03.040
And we demanded
BigQuery to scale up,

00:14:03.040 --> 00:14:06.410
because BigQuery is BigQuery.

00:14:06.410 --> 00:14:08.180
The issues that
we faced in there

00:14:08.180 --> 00:14:11.290
were, anytime we had to update
old data, it's a trouble.

00:14:11.290 --> 00:14:12.987
There are update
operations in BigQuery,

00:14:12.987 --> 00:14:14.570
but you don't really
want to do those.

00:14:14.570 --> 00:14:18.390
Those are really costly,
and limitations are there.

00:14:18.390 --> 00:14:20.570
Plus, every time we
were querying the data,

00:14:20.570 --> 00:14:22.197
we were querying
all of the data.

00:14:22.197 --> 00:14:23.780
So the way BigQuery
works is, whenever

00:14:23.780 --> 00:14:25.403
you are querying
a column, it has

00:14:25.403 --> 00:14:27.320
to go through all of the
values in that column

00:14:27.320 --> 00:14:31.580
unless you limit it via
some certain limitations.

00:14:31.580 --> 00:14:34.550
Plus, as we are storing
all of this data

00:14:34.550 --> 00:14:37.370
from years worth of their
campaigns, it keeps--

00:14:37.370 --> 00:14:39.320
the storage costs
keep getting add up.

00:14:39.320 --> 00:14:41.700
So we want to reduce that
storage cost as well.

00:14:41.700 --> 00:14:45.680
So as you can see here, a query
that ran on a small table,

00:14:45.680 --> 00:14:50.030
it cost us 32 MB to
process that data.

00:14:50.030 --> 00:14:52.220
MARTIN OMANDER: So
the 32 megabytes here

00:14:52.220 --> 00:14:55.458
is data that is pulled in?

00:14:55.458 --> 00:14:57.500
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah, just
because we are querying

00:14:57.500 --> 00:14:58.700
one of the columns in there.

00:14:58.700 --> 00:15:01.920
MARTIN OMANDER: Ah, and you are
not-- perhaps not even using--

00:15:01.920 --> 00:15:02.040
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah--

00:15:02.040 --> 00:15:02.480
MARTIN OMANDER:
--all of that data?

00:15:02.480 --> 00:15:04.490
VIKRAM TIWARI: --perhaps we only
want to use the first tendrils,

00:15:04.490 --> 00:15:05.930
because I want to
only understand

00:15:05.930 --> 00:15:07.310
what happened in the last hour.

00:15:07.310 --> 00:15:09.170
I don't need to get
all the data in there.

00:15:09.170 --> 00:15:11.490
But there's no way to put the
limits around that right now.

00:15:11.490 --> 00:15:12.532
MARTIN OMANDER: So I see.

00:15:12.532 --> 00:15:15.087
And you typically-- you
have time series data.

00:15:15.087 --> 00:15:15.920
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah.

00:15:15.920 --> 00:15:17.295
MARTIN OMANDER:
And you typically

00:15:17.295 --> 00:15:19.110
are more interested
in the newer data.

00:15:19.110 --> 00:15:19.630
VIKRAM TIWARI: Generally, yeah.

00:15:19.630 --> 00:15:19.850
MARTIN OMANDER: Generally.

00:15:19.850 --> 00:15:20.420
VIKRAM TIWARI:
Clients don't want

00:15:20.420 --> 00:15:22.070
to see the historical
data unless they

00:15:22.070 --> 00:15:24.360
were doing a meeting in
end of the month, yeah.

00:15:24.360 --> 00:15:25.580
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah,
yeah, and sometimes they

00:15:25.580 --> 00:15:26.245
want to see it all--

00:15:26.245 --> 00:15:26.550
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah.

00:15:26.550 --> 00:15:28.290
MARTIN OMANDER: --but most of
the time, just the new stuff.

00:15:28.290 --> 00:15:29.480
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah.

00:15:29.480 --> 00:15:31.910
MARTIN OMANDER: So
for that one, there

00:15:31.910 --> 00:15:35.160
is a technique called
date partitioning.

00:15:35.160 --> 00:15:38.870
So basically, you
have this big table.

00:15:38.870 --> 00:15:42.950
But within BigQuery, you can
tell BigQuery to partition

00:15:42.950 --> 00:15:45.020
on date, for example.

00:15:45.020 --> 00:15:48.620
And internally, BigQuery
sort of splits it up

00:15:48.620 --> 00:15:53.810
into many small tables so you
don't have to query them all.

00:15:53.810 --> 00:15:55.857
Would that be a good
fit for Omni Labs?

00:15:55.857 --> 00:15:56.690
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah.

00:15:56.690 --> 00:15:58.350
Yeah, so that's what
we ended up doing.

00:15:58.350 --> 00:16:00.780
We ended up with the approach
of divide and conquer.

00:16:00.780 --> 00:16:03.560
So we took our data
as it was coming in,

00:16:03.560 --> 00:16:05.500
we split it into the
date chunks as it

00:16:05.500 --> 00:16:08.300
is coming in based upon the
date that we got the data.

00:16:08.300 --> 00:16:10.250
We split it and put it
into that partition.

00:16:10.250 --> 00:16:14.240
And using that, we could
actually ingest a lot of data,

00:16:14.240 --> 00:16:15.710
even if it's a historical data.

00:16:15.710 --> 00:16:17.770
Earlier, we were like, we don't
want to ingest all this data,

00:16:17.770 --> 00:16:19.103
because it's going to be costly.

00:16:19.103 --> 00:16:20.720
Now we could ingest
all the data,

00:16:20.720 --> 00:16:25.410
because BigQuery allows
you to store all the data.

00:16:25.410 --> 00:16:26.930
And if you're not
querying on that,

00:16:26.930 --> 00:16:28.760
they reduce the
pricing for you--

00:16:28.760 --> 00:16:29.900
win.

00:16:29.900 --> 00:16:31.340
Who doesn't want that?

00:16:31.340 --> 00:16:32.690
It's an easy win.

00:16:32.690 --> 00:16:36.050
Plus, now that I'm querying
on these partition tables,

00:16:36.050 --> 00:16:39.420
I can very easily limit
those to a particular vendor.

00:16:39.420 --> 00:16:41.420
So as you can see
in the query, we

00:16:41.420 --> 00:16:44.563
are partitioning it
to just 2018 data.

00:16:44.563 --> 00:16:46.480
Even if I'm querying two
years' worth of data,

00:16:46.480 --> 00:16:50.370
I've already reduced the size
from 32 MBs of query to 9.7 MB

00:16:50.370 --> 00:16:51.040
of query.

00:16:51.040 --> 00:16:53.860
Query runs faster, gives
me the result faster,

00:16:53.860 --> 00:16:55.810
and the costs are cheap.

00:16:55.810 --> 00:16:58.390
So I'm saving not
only in the storage,

00:16:58.390 --> 00:17:01.195
I'm also saving on the
queries that I'm making.

00:17:01.195 --> 00:17:03.070
MARTIN OMANDER: And you
mentioned there was--

00:17:03.070 --> 00:17:06.859
this older data is
cheaper to store?

00:17:06.859 --> 00:17:09.730
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah,
yeah, so after 30 days--

00:17:09.730 --> 00:17:11.890
if I'm not getting
the number wrong--

00:17:11.890 --> 00:17:13.750
if you don't access the data--

00:17:13.750 --> 00:17:16.810
if you don't update the data--
you can still access the data.

00:17:16.810 --> 00:17:18.797
Accessing the data never gets--

00:17:18.797 --> 00:17:19.839
like, there's no latency.

00:17:19.839 --> 00:17:21.397
You won't see the latency there.

00:17:21.397 --> 00:17:23.230
But it just becomes
cheaper since you're not

00:17:23.230 --> 00:17:25.480
updating that data anymore.

00:17:25.480 --> 00:17:26.950
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, that's nice.

00:17:26.950 --> 00:17:27.790
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah.

00:17:27.790 --> 00:17:29.248
MARTIN OMANDER: So
we talked, here,

00:17:29.248 --> 00:17:32.440
about-- we heard about
partitioning by date,

00:17:32.440 --> 00:17:34.810
putting limitations on date.

00:17:34.810 --> 00:17:37.780
There is, of course, this
general purpose database

00:17:37.780 --> 00:17:41.590
that allows you to do all
that that is not BigQuery.

00:17:41.590 --> 00:17:47.260
So there is just plain, old
SQL databases you could run.

00:17:47.260 --> 00:17:51.755
And you can put limits on
when you run queries on those.

00:17:51.755 --> 00:17:54.130
Could you tell us a little
bit about why you're not going

00:17:54.130 --> 00:17:55.240
with a plain SQL solution?

00:17:55.240 --> 00:17:56.740
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah,
so SQL solution

00:17:56.740 --> 00:17:57.740
would have been perfect.

00:17:57.740 --> 00:18:00.257
Because what you can do is,
next day, when you get the data

00:18:00.257 --> 00:18:01.840
and you see the
numbers are different,

00:18:01.840 --> 00:18:03.880
you can just go and
update that number value.

00:18:03.880 --> 00:18:06.510
But SQL solutions are
really easy to get setup,

00:18:06.510 --> 00:18:07.842
but really hard to scale.

00:18:07.842 --> 00:18:10.300
Think of, right now, it could
have started with a small SQL

00:18:10.300 --> 00:18:11.120
instance.

00:18:11.120 --> 00:18:13.540
But as soon as I get more
data, I need more disk space.

00:18:13.540 --> 00:18:15.220
As soon as I have more
clients which are querying,

00:18:15.220 --> 00:18:16.990
I need to scale up
my cluster, because I

00:18:16.990 --> 00:18:18.100
need more RAM in there.

00:18:18.100 --> 00:18:19.390
I need more CPU.

00:18:19.390 --> 00:18:21.250
When there are a lot
of clients doing this,

00:18:21.250 --> 00:18:23.380
and they want to
do this frequently,

00:18:23.380 --> 00:18:25.960
I want to add a cache
in the front of that.

00:18:25.960 --> 00:18:27.910
And as clients
scale on that, you

00:18:27.910 --> 00:18:30.020
add a cluster in
front of that again.

00:18:30.020 --> 00:18:32.500
So it's just chasing the
problem all the way down.

00:18:32.500 --> 00:18:34.760
And you keep scaling,
and it never stops.

00:18:34.760 --> 00:18:36.910
With BigQuery, push
it all in there,

00:18:36.910 --> 00:18:39.330
don't have to worry
about it ever again.

00:18:39.330 --> 00:18:40.960
MARTIN OMANDER: And SQL--

00:18:40.960 --> 00:18:43.937
Cloud SQL is
somewhat serverful--

00:18:43.937 --> 00:18:44.770
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah.

00:18:44.770 --> 00:18:45.490
MARTIN OMANDER: --as well.

00:18:45.490 --> 00:18:46.930
VIKRAM TIWARI:
Yeah, so it's like,

00:18:46.930 --> 00:18:50.177
you have to still do the
capacity planning on your side.

00:18:50.177 --> 00:18:51.760
They can automatically
do replication.

00:18:51.760 --> 00:18:53.200
They will do backups for you.

00:18:53.200 --> 00:18:55.460
They will manage it
as a SQL database.

00:18:55.460 --> 00:18:56.770
It's not an analytics database.

00:18:56.770 --> 00:18:58.960
You want to use BigQuery
as the analytics database

00:18:58.960 --> 00:19:00.778
for your workloads, yeah.

00:19:00.778 --> 00:19:02.320
MARTIN OMANDER: And
you said, Vikram,

00:19:02.320 --> 00:19:04.270
that it's easy to get started.

00:19:04.270 --> 00:19:05.103
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah.

00:19:05.103 --> 00:19:08.050
MARTIN OMANDER: So is this
something that, perhaps,

00:19:08.050 --> 00:19:10.030
when you start building
a proof of concept,

00:19:10.030 --> 00:19:11.010
you might go with SQL--

00:19:11.010 --> 00:19:11.230
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah.

00:19:11.230 --> 00:19:12.670
MARTIN OMANDER: --and
then migrate to BigQuery?

00:19:12.670 --> 00:19:13.060
Or--

00:19:13.060 --> 00:19:13.300
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah.

00:19:13.300 --> 00:19:13.930
MARTIN OMANDER: --what
are your thoughts?

00:19:13.930 --> 00:19:15.250
VIKRAM TIWARI: Depending
upon your needs--

00:19:15.250 --> 00:19:16.870
if your needs are not really--

00:19:16.870 --> 00:19:19.050
if you see that your clients
are not going to be--

00:19:19.050 --> 00:19:20.950
it's either not
too many clients,

00:19:20.950 --> 00:19:23.680
or it's not going to be too
many frequent queries, or not

00:19:23.680 --> 00:19:26.413
too many aggregations, SQL
might be a perfect fit for you.

00:19:26.413 --> 00:19:28.330
And you might have to
maybe go to two or three

00:19:28.330 --> 00:19:31.002
tiers of scaling of
your SQL instances.

00:19:31.002 --> 00:19:31.960
And you should do that.

00:19:31.960 --> 00:19:33.640
It's very easy to get started.

00:19:33.640 --> 00:19:36.100
But BigQuery is also easy.

00:19:36.100 --> 00:19:37.143
It scales.

00:19:37.143 --> 00:19:38.185
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, good.

00:19:38.185 --> 00:19:39.018
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah.

00:19:39.018 --> 00:19:40.890
MARTIN OMANDER: Excellent.

00:19:40.890 --> 00:19:44.410
All right, those were
two down, two to go.

00:19:44.410 --> 00:19:48.460
The third item, the third
engineering challenge,

00:19:48.460 --> 00:19:51.460
is keeping the machine
learning models fresh.

00:19:51.460 --> 00:19:55.420
Because as we heard, in
a machine learning lab,

00:19:55.420 --> 00:19:57.970
or when you're testing it
out, you might build a model,

00:19:57.970 --> 00:19:59.240
and you're done.

00:19:59.240 --> 00:20:02.410
But in a real company,
like Vikram's company,

00:20:02.410 --> 00:20:05.170
these models have to be
refreshed all the time

00:20:05.170 --> 00:20:08.230
for the new data that comes in.

00:20:08.230 --> 00:20:12.540
So these models must
be updated all the time

00:20:12.540 --> 00:20:13.800
on the latest data.

00:20:13.800 --> 00:20:15.600
Could you tell us some
about the challenges

00:20:15.600 --> 00:20:16.980
you run into when updating?

00:20:16.980 --> 00:20:18.570
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah, sure.

00:20:18.570 --> 00:20:21.330
So think of like, when you
build a machine learning model,

00:20:21.330 --> 00:20:22.230
it uses--

00:20:22.230 --> 00:20:25.230
we used last year worth of
data to build that model.

00:20:25.230 --> 00:20:27.270
Now, next day, I'm
getting new data,

00:20:27.270 --> 00:20:29.190
which is only 24 hours of data.

00:20:29.190 --> 00:20:32.340
But [INAUDIBLE],, I want to make
sure that my model is fresh.

00:20:32.340 --> 00:20:35.520
So I will have to use this one
year worth of data plus this 24

00:20:35.520 --> 00:20:38.220
hours of data merged
together and then retrain

00:20:38.220 --> 00:20:39.480
my whole model.

00:20:39.480 --> 00:20:40.050
This works.

00:20:40.050 --> 00:20:42.420
But every few days, you're
going to keep increasing

00:20:42.420 --> 00:20:43.720
the size of your data.

00:20:43.720 --> 00:20:45.940
So the retraining time
will-- every few days,

00:20:45.940 --> 00:20:47.250
you will see it increasing.

00:20:47.250 --> 00:20:48.800
And there's no way around it.

00:20:48.800 --> 00:20:50.100
Or is it?

00:20:50.100 --> 00:20:51.900
There is a way.

00:20:51.900 --> 00:20:55.880
So we'll talk about
the way in a minute.

00:20:55.880 --> 00:20:58.315
Another issue that
we had was we don't

00:20:58.315 --> 00:21:00.690
want to keep rebuilding these
models for every new client

00:21:00.690 --> 00:21:01.680
that we get.

00:21:01.680 --> 00:21:03.720
If all the clients
that we are getting

00:21:03.720 --> 00:21:07.170
are trying to do the same
problem over and over again,

00:21:07.170 --> 00:21:09.270
you don't want to rebuild
the same model code

00:21:09.270 --> 00:21:11.640
and redeploy those same
pipelines all the time.

00:21:11.640 --> 00:21:13.230
You want to use
the same version--

00:21:13.230 --> 00:21:16.830
same model-- retrain that
model, and utilize that model

00:21:16.830 --> 00:21:18.960
for all the clients if you can.

00:21:18.960 --> 00:21:22.020
So what we want to use
is a central model,

00:21:22.020 --> 00:21:26.510
want to retrain that for the
specific data for the client.

00:21:26.510 --> 00:21:30.400
So our initial approach
was using XGBoost.

00:21:30.400 --> 00:21:33.100
XGBoost allows us to
build linear models

00:21:33.100 --> 00:21:35.230
or random forest-type models.

00:21:35.230 --> 00:21:38.480
And you can very easily
scale the parameters in here.

00:21:38.480 --> 00:21:40.630
So as we got more
clients, as we got

00:21:40.630 --> 00:21:44.080
more different types of clients,
or different types of options

00:21:44.080 --> 00:21:47.020
that they wanted to include,
we built a lot of models,

00:21:47.020 --> 00:21:48.770
and the model size
was increasing.

00:21:48.770 --> 00:21:51.550
So as we see here, initially,
we started with-- on week 1,

00:21:51.550 --> 00:21:52.870
we have a smaller model.

00:21:52.870 --> 00:21:55.540
On week 2, we get-- model
size keeps getting bigger.

00:21:55.540 --> 00:21:57.160
And data also keeps
getting bigger.

00:21:57.160 --> 00:21:58.900
The compute time
takes-- getting bigger.

00:21:58.900 --> 00:22:01.780
Everything gets-- keeps
getting bigger and bigger.

00:22:01.780 --> 00:22:04.120
Model retraining also
takes a lot of time.

00:22:04.120 --> 00:22:05.800
Our clients are not
satisfied if you

00:22:05.800 --> 00:22:10.150
take more than 24 hours just to
give them real-time predictions

00:22:10.150 --> 00:22:12.120
on the new data.

00:22:12.120 --> 00:22:15.000
Plus, we have to manually
maintain the whole model

00:22:15.000 --> 00:22:16.590
deployment lifecycle.

00:22:16.590 --> 00:22:18.150
Every time we get
a new model, you

00:22:18.150 --> 00:22:20.407
have to make sure that the
older model gets fazed out.

00:22:20.407 --> 00:22:22.740
You want to deallocate the
resources, compute resources,

00:22:22.740 --> 00:22:23.883
that are available there.

00:22:23.883 --> 00:22:25.050
Maybe you want to keep them.

00:22:25.050 --> 00:22:27.090
Because maybe the new model
doesn't work really well,

00:22:27.090 --> 00:22:28.840
and you want to really
quickly switch back

00:22:28.840 --> 00:22:30.155
to the older version as well.

00:22:30.155 --> 00:22:32.100
So there's a lot of
toil that goes in there

00:22:32.100 --> 00:22:34.575
in maintaining these models.

00:22:34.575 --> 00:22:36.950
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah, I guess
you could-- theoretically--

00:22:36.950 --> 00:22:42.380
end up in a place where it takes
more than 24 hours to process

00:22:42.380 --> 00:22:43.340
the last 24 hours--

00:22:43.340 --> 00:22:43.870
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah.

00:22:43.870 --> 00:22:45.240
MARTIN OMANDER: --which
means you never catch up.

00:22:45.240 --> 00:22:46.698
VIKRAM TIWARI:
Yeah, and it happens

00:22:46.698 --> 00:22:49.380
as soon as you have more clients
and a lot of data coming in.

00:22:49.380 --> 00:22:51.030
And you will face that problem.

00:22:51.030 --> 00:22:52.730
[LAUGHTER] Yeah.

00:22:52.730 --> 00:22:54.920
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, that
sounds like a good problem.

00:22:54.920 --> 00:22:55.580
VIKRAM TIWARI: It's
a good problem.

00:22:55.580 --> 00:22:55.910
MARTIN OMANDER: Lots of clients!

00:22:55.910 --> 00:22:58.400
VIKRAM TIWARI: I don't
mind the problem.

00:22:58.400 --> 00:22:59.270
Yeah.

00:22:59.270 --> 00:23:02.660
MARTIN OMANDER:
Yeah, so one thing

00:23:02.660 --> 00:23:04.280
to consider in this
sort of situation

00:23:04.280 --> 00:23:07.370
is TensorFlow-- running
TensorFlow on ML Engine, which

00:23:07.370 --> 00:23:08.870
is serverless.

00:23:08.870 --> 00:23:11.210
You don't have to
set up servers.

00:23:11.210 --> 00:23:12.830
It scales better.

00:23:12.830 --> 00:23:15.800
You get these check
points and API

00:23:15.800 --> 00:23:18.560
versioning that Vikram
was just talking

00:23:18.560 --> 00:23:20.220
about-- you get those built in.

00:23:20.220 --> 00:23:22.852
So you don't have to
manually deal with them.

00:23:22.852 --> 00:23:24.560
And of course-- because
it's serverless--

00:23:24.560 --> 00:23:28.910
you can then focus on
your models and not ops.

00:23:28.910 --> 00:23:31.310
Is that something that
would fit for Omni Labs?

00:23:31.310 --> 00:23:33.188
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah,
so we ended up actually

00:23:33.188 --> 00:23:34.730
using TensorFlow on
Machine Learning.

00:23:34.730 --> 00:23:36.590
So rather than using
XGBoost models,

00:23:36.590 --> 00:23:38.597
we ended up moving all
of that to TensorFlow.

00:23:38.597 --> 00:23:40.430
With TensorFlow, it
provides you the ability

00:23:40.430 --> 00:23:42.890
to retrain your models,
only on the new data.

00:23:42.890 --> 00:23:44.120
We use checkpointing.

00:23:44.120 --> 00:23:45.872
With checkpointing,
you train your model

00:23:45.872 --> 00:23:47.330
for the one year
worth of data, you

00:23:47.330 --> 00:23:48.740
create a checkpoint
on that time.

00:23:48.740 --> 00:23:51.000
And next time, when you
have to retrain your data--

00:23:51.000 --> 00:23:52.690
your model again today--

00:23:52.690 --> 00:23:55.070
you get the new data,
start from the checkpoint,

00:23:55.070 --> 00:23:57.160
and you retrain your model
on the new data only.

00:23:57.160 --> 00:23:58.760
Training time is much faster.

00:23:58.760 --> 00:24:00.902
The only time we have
to take longer time

00:24:00.902 --> 00:24:02.360
is when a new client
comes on board

00:24:02.360 --> 00:24:05.020
and we have to retrain the
model on the whole of the data.

00:24:05.020 --> 00:24:06.530
Then another that we had--

00:24:06.530 --> 00:24:09.140
retraining for all
the different clients.

00:24:09.140 --> 00:24:11.300
Now with TensorFlow,
there's a concept therein,

00:24:11.300 --> 00:24:12.710
called transfer learning.

00:24:12.710 --> 00:24:15.980
So we built one big model,
and anytime a new client comes

00:24:15.980 --> 00:24:18.950
in, we use transfer learning
to transfer the parameters

00:24:18.950 --> 00:24:21.680
and weights that we have
learned-- across our clients--

00:24:21.680 --> 00:24:23.180
on to specific.

00:24:23.180 --> 00:24:24.830
Based upon the needs
and constraints

00:24:24.830 --> 00:24:27.170
of a specific client,
we retrain that model.

00:24:27.170 --> 00:24:28.550
And now we have a new model.

00:24:28.550 --> 00:24:30.740
And we can keep retraining
our global model

00:24:30.740 --> 00:24:34.630
and split off a model
for each client based

00:24:34.630 --> 00:24:36.320
upon their own data.

00:24:36.320 --> 00:24:39.180
Plus we ended up using a wide
and deep model for this--

00:24:39.180 --> 00:24:40.650
I'll talk about
that in a minute.

00:24:40.650 --> 00:24:42.950
The best point with this
was we reduced the cost

00:24:42.950 --> 00:24:47.000
so much that we don't really
see any increase happening.

00:24:47.000 --> 00:24:48.922
It's always a flat line.

00:24:48.922 --> 00:24:50.630
MARTIN OMANDER: And
does this mean, then,

00:24:50.630 --> 00:24:53.870
that if I come on as
a new customer, maybe

00:24:53.870 --> 00:24:56.340
I don't have a whole lot of
data I'm bringing with me--

00:24:56.340 --> 00:24:57.173
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah.

00:24:57.173 --> 00:24:59.930
MARTIN OMANDER: You can still
serve decent predictions

00:24:59.930 --> 00:25:03.930
because you have a good model
for all your other clients.

00:25:03.930 --> 00:25:06.290
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah, so this
helps new clients as well.

00:25:06.290 --> 00:25:08.540
Because new clients haven't
tried all the geographies.

00:25:08.540 --> 00:25:10.790
New clients haven't tried
all the different parameters

00:25:10.790 --> 00:25:13.520
that they can run
their campaigns upon.

00:25:13.520 --> 00:25:15.830
Since we have seen this
across our clients,

00:25:15.830 --> 00:25:18.650
we can give them optimizations
and recommendations

00:25:18.650 --> 00:25:22.340
on how to run their campaigns
because we see a full picture.

00:25:22.340 --> 00:25:25.187
And we don't have to wait
for them to get their data.

00:25:25.187 --> 00:25:26.270
We know their constraints.

00:25:26.270 --> 00:25:28.637
We can retrain the model
based on their constraints.

00:25:28.637 --> 00:25:30.470
MARTIN OMANDER: So the
more clients you get,

00:25:30.470 --> 00:25:32.387
the smarter your
company becomes.

00:25:32.387 --> 00:25:33.970
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah,
it feeds on data.

00:25:33.970 --> 00:25:35.803
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah,
[? virtuous ?] cycle--

00:25:35.803 --> 00:25:36.310
I like that.

00:25:36.310 --> 00:25:39.380
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah, so I talked
about wide and deep models.

00:25:39.380 --> 00:25:45.200
When we started moving away
from XGBoost to TensorFlow,

00:25:45.200 --> 00:25:46.790
we stumbled into this.

00:25:46.790 --> 00:25:48.830
This was very recent
at that point in time.

00:25:48.830 --> 00:25:52.500
Wide models are really
good for memorizing stuff.

00:25:52.500 --> 00:25:55.550
So if you want to make
sure that whenever

00:25:55.550 --> 00:25:57.543
you are doing a targeting
on and single place,

00:25:57.543 --> 00:25:59.960
you want to make sure when you
are targeting San Francisco

00:25:59.960 --> 00:26:01.550
you also get Oakland in there.

00:26:01.550 --> 00:26:03.383
So those models will
be really good at that,

00:26:03.383 --> 00:26:05.940
because they understand this
whole thing is the Bay Area.

00:26:05.940 --> 00:26:08.560
Deep models are really
good at generalizing.

00:26:08.560 --> 00:26:10.585
So if you want to
target San Francisco,

00:26:10.585 --> 00:26:12.210
they might also
suggest to you that you

00:26:12.210 --> 00:26:13.620
should try Austin and New York.

00:26:13.620 --> 00:26:15.390
Because those are
tech hubs, and it

00:26:15.390 --> 00:26:18.762
has learned that
because often those

00:26:18.762 --> 00:26:20.970
are deep neural networks--
because of generalization,

00:26:20.970 --> 00:26:22.680
their ability to generalize.

00:26:22.680 --> 00:26:25.320
But what happens with
deep neural networks

00:26:25.320 --> 00:26:27.390
is those are not really
constraint-specific.

00:26:27.390 --> 00:26:30.420
You can't put some
constraints in there to say,

00:26:30.420 --> 00:26:32.490
I actually don't
want to use this.

00:26:32.490 --> 00:26:36.180
So an option is if you
want to get recommendations

00:26:36.180 --> 00:26:39.540
for teenagers, you don't really
want adult recommendations

00:26:39.540 --> 00:26:40.150
in there.

00:26:40.150 --> 00:26:41.442
You just want it for teenagers.

00:26:41.442 --> 00:26:43.860
So you want to put those
limitations in there.

00:26:43.860 --> 00:26:46.360
For these kinds of
problems that we have--

00:26:46.360 --> 00:26:47.670
we have similar problems.

00:26:47.670 --> 00:26:50.320
For this, we ended up
merging these two together.

00:26:50.320 --> 00:26:53.550
This is not our research--
this was just by Google team.

00:26:53.550 --> 00:26:54.990
We used wide and deep models.

00:26:54.990 --> 00:26:56.720
There are papers
published for this.

00:26:56.720 --> 00:26:59.820
And to do this whole thing, we
could have written whole code

00:26:59.820 --> 00:27:00.600
from our side.

00:27:00.600 --> 00:27:02.880
But TensorFlow
provides an easy way

00:27:02.880 --> 00:27:04.710
to utilize wide and deep models.

00:27:04.710 --> 00:27:06.180
They have the
fully-featured API.

00:27:06.180 --> 00:27:08.640
All you have to do is
connect your data into it.

00:27:08.640 --> 00:27:11.113
You can optimize and
tell it exactly which

00:27:11.113 --> 00:27:12.780
features are the cross
features that you

00:27:12.780 --> 00:27:15.090
want to utilize-- which ones are
the deep features that you want

00:27:15.090 --> 00:27:15.590
to utilize.

00:27:15.590 --> 00:27:17.130
You can put your
constants in there.

00:27:17.130 --> 00:27:18.900
Plus, you don't
have to manage how

00:27:18.900 --> 00:27:21.680
the data will get distributed--
how that training will happen.

00:27:21.680 --> 00:27:23.580
TensorFlow will take
care of all of that.

00:27:23.580 --> 00:27:25.380
If you put it on
Machine Learning Engine,

00:27:25.380 --> 00:27:27.780
it will take care of scaling
the machines for you.

00:27:27.780 --> 00:27:30.310
And you can do hyperparameter
tuning as well there.

00:27:30.310 --> 00:27:33.780
So you get the best
model architecture

00:27:33.780 --> 00:27:35.190
by using wide and deep.

00:27:35.190 --> 00:27:37.260
And you get the
best model accuracy

00:27:37.260 --> 00:27:39.600
because of using
hyperparameter tuning

00:27:39.600 --> 00:27:40.860
on Cloud [? ML ?] Engine.

00:27:40.860 --> 00:27:42.900
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, so it's kind
of the best of both worlds--

00:27:42.900 --> 00:27:43.230
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah.

00:27:43.230 --> 00:27:43.980
MARTIN OMANDER: --if
you combine them.

00:27:43.980 --> 00:27:44.813
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah.

00:27:44.813 --> 00:27:46.440
MARTIN OMANDER: Really nice.

00:27:46.440 --> 00:27:49.710
All right, so all of this
shuffling data around--

00:27:49.710 --> 00:27:53.670
all of this tuning and models.

00:27:53.670 --> 00:27:56.430
All of this stuff
accounts for nothing

00:27:56.430 --> 00:28:01.870
if you aren't actually able to
make predictions in the end.

00:28:01.870 --> 00:28:05.470
So making predictions--
I understand that this is

00:28:05.470 --> 00:28:06.610
something where--

00:28:06.610 --> 00:28:09.280
you told me customers
come in on Monday morning.

00:28:09.280 --> 00:28:10.240
They all sit there.

00:28:10.240 --> 00:28:11.357
They all hit the report.

00:28:11.357 --> 00:28:12.190
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah.

00:28:12.190 --> 00:28:13.950
MARTIN OMANDER: And then
what used to happen?

00:28:13.950 --> 00:28:14.003
What--

00:28:14.003 --> 00:28:14.056
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah.

00:28:14.056 --> 00:28:15.210
MARTIN OMANDER:
--was the challenge?

00:28:15.210 --> 00:28:17.335
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah, so
the challenge that we faced

00:28:17.335 --> 00:28:19.370
is nobody cares about
their campaigns on Friday.

00:28:19.370 --> 00:28:21.160
Everybody wants their
reports on Monday morning.

00:28:21.160 --> 00:28:22.450
And they want all
the suggestions,

00:28:22.450 --> 00:28:24.760
because they want to apply
all of the suggestions now.

00:28:24.760 --> 00:28:27.130
So every Monday,
in the morning, we

00:28:27.130 --> 00:28:28.750
will see a lot of
traffic come in.

00:28:28.750 --> 00:28:30.400
MARTIN OMANDER: You couldn't
tell them that, hey you guys,

00:28:30.400 --> 00:28:31.210
you start on Tuesday.

00:28:31.210 --> 00:28:32.320
[LAUGHTER] You
start on Wednesday.

00:28:32.320 --> 00:28:33.640
VIKRAM TIWARI: We
wish, like [INAUDIBLE],,

00:28:33.640 --> 00:28:35.050
you can only start
in the morning--

00:28:35.050 --> 00:28:35.410
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah, yeah.

00:28:35.410 --> 00:28:36.190
VIKRAM TIWARI: --at 10:00.

00:28:36.190 --> 00:28:37.720
MARTIN OMANDER: They all
wanted it Monday morning.

00:28:37.720 --> 00:28:38.140
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah.

00:28:38.140 --> 00:28:40.270
Everybody wants it in the
morning at the same time.

00:28:40.270 --> 00:28:40.558
MARTIN OMANDER:
At the same time.

00:28:40.558 --> 00:28:42.250
VIKRAM TIWARI: And that puts
a lot of load on your servers,

00:28:42.250 --> 00:28:43.810
no matter how much you scale.

00:28:43.810 --> 00:28:46.170
And scaling-- time
to talk about that.

00:28:46.170 --> 00:28:46.670
OK.

00:28:46.670 --> 00:28:46.970
MARTIN OMANDER: Mm-hmm.

00:28:46.970 --> 00:28:48.670
VIKRAM TIWARI: So the
approach that we went with--

00:28:48.670 --> 00:28:50.420
OK, if we are seeing
more traffic come in,

00:28:50.420 --> 00:28:52.300
let's increase the machine size.

00:28:52.300 --> 00:28:54.550
Let's add more machines
into this cluster.

00:28:54.550 --> 00:28:57.280
As we see more and
more users come in,

00:28:57.280 --> 00:28:58.910
we kept increasing
those machines.

00:28:58.910 --> 00:29:00.850
But it's not really
possible all the time.

00:29:00.850 --> 00:29:03.080
Because you can keep
increasing the machines,

00:29:03.080 --> 00:29:07.430
but from Thursday to Sunday
evening, nobody is using those.

00:29:07.430 --> 00:29:08.680
Nobody is creating on those.

00:29:08.680 --> 00:29:11.470
So all the machines that
you have scaled up there,

00:29:11.470 --> 00:29:13.440
you're just paying--

00:29:13.440 --> 00:29:14.560
I don't mind paying them.

00:29:14.560 --> 00:29:16.020
But I don't want to
pay them all the time.

00:29:16.020 --> 00:29:17.270
MARTIN OMANDER: Please pay us.

00:29:17.270 --> 00:29:17.880
Yes.

00:29:17.880 --> 00:29:20.040
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah, what
would happen, though,

00:29:20.040 --> 00:29:23.370
on Monday morning, no matter
how much we had scaled it up,

00:29:23.370 --> 00:29:25.830
it would still go to 100% CPU,
because everybody's trying

00:29:25.830 --> 00:29:27.520
to do it at the same time.

00:29:27.520 --> 00:29:29.490
Everybody is
creating the servers.

00:29:29.490 --> 00:29:31.132
And machines would
become unresponsive.

00:29:31.132 --> 00:29:33.090
You don't really want
your machines to go down.

00:29:33.090 --> 00:29:34.560
All the hard work that we did--

00:29:34.560 --> 00:29:36.270
on getting that data,
transforming it,

00:29:36.270 --> 00:29:39.120
putting it everywhere, building
these machine learning models--

00:29:39.120 --> 00:29:41.550
all of that fails if you
are not able to serve

00:29:41.550 --> 00:29:43.053
your clients properly.

00:29:43.053 --> 00:29:44.970
MARTIN OMANDER: So what
would actually happen?

00:29:44.970 --> 00:29:46.530
Would they crash?

00:29:46.530 --> 00:29:47.737
Would they slow down?

00:29:47.737 --> 00:29:48.570
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah.

00:29:48.570 --> 00:29:49.160
MARTIN OMANDER:
What would you see?

00:29:49.160 --> 00:29:49.950
VIKRAM TIWARI: So
it starts with--

00:29:49.950 --> 00:29:51.735
the first person who
gets to the query

00:29:51.735 --> 00:29:53.110
gets their result
really quickly.

00:29:53.110 --> 00:29:53.250
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah.

00:29:53.250 --> 00:29:55.090
VIKRAM TIWARI: But after
that, it slowly and gradually

00:29:55.090 --> 00:29:56.280
starts slowing down.

00:29:56.280 --> 00:29:56.790
MARTIN OMANDER: Uh-huh.

00:29:56.790 --> 00:29:59.332
VIKRAM TIWARI: Unless we see
it, and we increase the machines

00:29:59.332 --> 00:30:00.000
in the cluster.

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:03.240
But it gradually slows down,
and once the CPU reaches 100%,

00:30:03.240 --> 00:30:04.470
it just becomes unresponsive.

00:30:04.470 --> 00:30:06.128
Nobody's getting any
predictions today.

00:30:06.128 --> 00:30:07.920
MARTIN OMANDER: Nobody
is getting anything.

00:30:07.920 --> 00:30:08.753
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah.

00:30:08.753 --> 00:30:10.230
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, yeah--

00:30:10.230 --> 00:30:10.860
no good.

00:30:10.860 --> 00:30:14.610
OK, so it sounds like a
very variable load here.

00:30:14.610 --> 00:30:16.620
And a lot of systems have that.

00:30:16.620 --> 00:30:19.080
I mean most systems
that all of us build

00:30:19.080 --> 00:30:21.450
are probably very variable.

00:30:21.450 --> 00:30:24.660
Not very many people are hitting
our stuff at 3:00 AM, right?

00:30:24.660 --> 00:30:26.620
But there are more
people hitting it, say,

00:30:26.620 --> 00:30:28.420
Monday morning.

00:30:28.420 --> 00:30:32.100
So one possibility
here is to let

00:30:32.100 --> 00:30:36.060
Google handle the scaling up
and down with Cloud Functions.

00:30:36.060 --> 00:30:40.050
Cloud Functions is
sort of a gateway--

00:30:40.050 --> 00:30:43.980
it can be one of your gateways
into the Google Cloud.

00:30:43.980 --> 00:30:49.080
Once the user hits a Cloud
Function endpoint-- an HTTP--

00:30:49.080 --> 00:30:51.630
you can now code, in
there, that they can then

00:30:51.630 --> 00:30:55.110
hit Cloud Storage, Machine
Learning, Bigtable--

00:30:55.110 --> 00:30:58.610
all these other products that
are in the Cloud Platform.

00:30:58.610 --> 00:31:00.210
And that ingestion point--

00:31:00.210 --> 00:31:05.220
that Cloud Function is
that gateway into GCP.

00:31:05.220 --> 00:31:08.520
It scales up and
also scales down,

00:31:08.520 --> 00:31:11.820
so you don't pay when
people aren't using it.

00:31:11.820 --> 00:31:14.520
Would that be a good fit for
this Monday morning problem?

00:31:14.520 --> 00:31:15.360
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah.

00:31:15.360 --> 00:31:19.210
So with Cloud Functions,
there's no server management.

00:31:19.210 --> 00:31:21.270
It automatically
scales up and down.

00:31:21.270 --> 00:31:23.520
You only pay when you
are running your code.

00:31:23.520 --> 00:31:25.440
And it runs in
response to the events.

00:31:25.440 --> 00:31:26.880
That's the best part.

00:31:26.880 --> 00:31:30.360
Plus, it connects and extends
to all of the existing Google

00:31:30.360 --> 00:31:34.050
Cloud Functions that we have--
all the internal systems,

00:31:34.050 --> 00:31:38.080
either Machine Learning Engine
or any other APIs that we have.

00:31:38.080 --> 00:31:40.410
So that's what we
ended up doing.

00:31:40.410 --> 00:31:42.472
Our new approach was
to go serverless.

00:31:42.472 --> 00:31:43.680
We went with Cloud Functions.

00:31:43.680 --> 00:31:47.100
Our error rate and latency
dropped to almost zero.

00:31:47.100 --> 00:31:49.530
It's still an error,
because users are now

00:31:49.530 --> 00:31:53.210
trying to get predictions
on very different data

00:31:53.210 --> 00:31:55.550
or something that our
model doesn't exist even.

00:31:55.550 --> 00:31:57.030
Or a totally new
client who wants

00:31:57.030 --> 00:31:59.197
to get their predictions
just right now without even

00:31:59.197 --> 00:32:00.350
running any campaigns.

00:32:00.350 --> 00:32:02.460
And people do that.

00:32:02.460 --> 00:32:04.230
So we were able to scale up.

00:32:04.230 --> 00:32:05.940
On Monday mornings,
as the load came in,

00:32:05.940 --> 00:32:07.480
it will automatically scale up.

00:32:07.480 --> 00:32:09.360
And whenever there is
no more requests coming

00:32:09.360 --> 00:32:11.280
in-- on Thursday until Sunday--

00:32:11.280 --> 00:32:13.810
it will just scale down itself.

00:32:13.810 --> 00:32:16.750
To write that code, it's
really simple actually.

00:32:16.750 --> 00:32:21.950
It's one single file that we
use across all of our clients.

00:32:21.950 --> 00:32:25.270
What we do is whenever
a different client comes

00:32:25.270 --> 00:32:29.073
in, all of those model names
and authentication parameters--

00:32:29.073 --> 00:32:30.490
along with the
data that they want

00:32:30.490 --> 00:32:32.500
to request to get
predictions upon--

00:32:32.500 --> 00:32:35.350
all of those get passed
into one single endpoint.

00:32:35.350 --> 00:32:37.530
And based upon that, we
make the correct request

00:32:37.530 --> 00:32:39.850
to Machine Learning Engine
to get the predictions.

00:32:39.850 --> 00:32:44.038
And one single point enables
all of this scale up and down.

00:32:44.038 --> 00:32:45.580
We don't have to
manage all the code.

00:32:45.580 --> 00:32:47.860
It's just one single
piece of code to manage.

00:32:47.860 --> 00:32:52.040
We don't-- error handling is in
there, because errors happen.

00:32:52.040 --> 00:32:52.540
Yeah.

00:32:52.540 --> 00:32:53.915
MARTIN OMANDER:
Errors do happen.

00:32:53.915 --> 00:32:57.100
VIKRAM TIWARI: So to wrap up--

00:32:57.100 --> 00:33:00.040
initially this was
our approach where

00:33:00.040 --> 00:33:01.755
we were hoping for no alerts.

00:33:01.755 --> 00:33:03.880
You can hope for that, but
you will still get that.

00:33:03.880 --> 00:33:05.470
As you can see, our
failover replica

00:33:05.470 --> 00:33:07.720
was also getting
100% usage in there.

00:33:07.720 --> 00:33:10.150
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, you
had a failover replica.

00:33:10.150 --> 00:33:10.983
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah.

00:33:10.983 --> 00:33:13.236
MARTIN OMANDER: And
the failover fell over.

00:33:13.236 --> 00:33:14.050
[LAUGHTER]

00:33:14.050 --> 00:33:15.640
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah, you can
never have too much compute.

00:33:15.640 --> 00:33:16.150
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah, yeah.

00:33:16.150 --> 00:33:17.650
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah,
so we started--

00:33:17.650 --> 00:33:20.620
we had Compute Engine for
our serving instances.

00:33:20.620 --> 00:33:23.827
We had Compute for our
Machine Learning tasks.

00:33:23.827 --> 00:33:25.910
We had Compute for our
data [? injection ?] tasks.

00:33:25.910 --> 00:33:27.920
Everywhere, it was
Compute Engine.

00:33:27.920 --> 00:33:32.770
But we moved from there into a
completely serverless solution

00:33:32.770 --> 00:33:36.200
where we focus only upon what
matters for our business.

00:33:36.200 --> 00:33:39.630
So clients now query
to Cloud Functions.

00:33:39.630 --> 00:33:43.690
Cloud Functions get the
predictions from ML Engine.

00:33:43.690 --> 00:33:46.240
And on the other
side, we query and get

00:33:46.240 --> 00:33:48.520
all the raw data
from external APIs,

00:33:48.520 --> 00:33:53.070
using Cloud Dataflow, post it to
BigQuery, and Google Storage--

00:33:53.070 --> 00:33:55.832
Cloud Storage for our machine
learning modeling tasks.

00:33:55.832 --> 00:33:57.290
MARTIN OMANDER:
Oh, it's all right.

00:33:57.290 --> 00:33:57.580
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah.

00:33:57.580 --> 00:33:59.288
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah,
I was going to say,

00:33:59.288 --> 00:34:01.090
you make it sound
very easy here-- yeah,

00:34:01.090 --> 00:34:04.270
we were used to it
this way, and 100% CPU.

00:34:04.270 --> 00:34:06.370
And we just switched over.

00:34:06.370 --> 00:34:07.990
I mean, you're a
smart guy, Vikram,

00:34:07.990 --> 00:34:11.620
but could you tell us a
little bit about your journey

00:34:11.620 --> 00:34:14.537
from the server-full world
to the serverless world.

00:34:14.537 --> 00:34:15.370
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah.

00:34:15.370 --> 00:34:17.270
MARTIN OMANDER:
It may have been--

00:34:17.270 --> 00:34:17.449
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah.

00:34:17.449 --> 00:34:18.782
MARTIN OMANDER: --a lot of work.

00:34:18.782 --> 00:34:22.219
VIKRAM TIWARI: Sure, so yes,
serverless works really well.

00:34:22.219 --> 00:34:24.110
But the best thing to
do-- that you can do--

00:34:24.110 --> 00:34:26.659
is trust the Google, or any
other serverless solution,

00:34:26.659 --> 00:34:28.190
but verify on your end.

00:34:28.190 --> 00:34:29.900
And no, you don't
need blockchain.

00:34:29.900 --> 00:34:32.929
So to do this, the
best way to get set up

00:34:32.929 --> 00:34:35.179
is set up your
monitoring and alerts.

00:34:35.179 --> 00:34:37.179
You want to make sure
that your services are up.

00:34:37.179 --> 00:34:38.637
And you are constantly
monitoring--

00:34:38.637 --> 00:34:39.710
like we did for BigQuery.

00:34:39.710 --> 00:34:42.320
We wanted to monitor how
our queries were doing.

00:34:42.320 --> 00:34:45.225
So we are looking at the latency
for our BigQuery queries.

00:34:45.225 --> 00:34:46.850
So monitoring and
alerts will make sure

00:34:46.850 --> 00:34:48.230
that your systems are up.

00:34:48.230 --> 00:34:51.139
And they are actually doing as
you would expect them to do.

00:34:51.139 --> 00:34:54.739
But sometimes, you actually
need a view into the systems.

00:34:54.739 --> 00:34:55.940
The systems are black boxes.

00:34:55.940 --> 00:34:58.505
You can't really SSH into
these machines and [? tail ?]

00:34:58.505 --> 00:34:59.330
the logs.

00:34:59.330 --> 00:35:00.747
So what you want
to do is you want

00:35:00.747 --> 00:35:02.930
to set up the systems in
there so you can actually

00:35:02.930 --> 00:35:05.120
get the logs in the real time.

00:35:05.120 --> 00:35:07.340
So we use Stackdriver
Logging, which

00:35:07.340 --> 00:35:09.980
enables us to get all the
logs from all these clusters

00:35:09.980 --> 00:35:11.522
that automatically
scale up and down.

00:35:11.522 --> 00:35:13.147
You don't have to do
anything in there.

00:35:13.147 --> 00:35:15.140
And it will just get all
these logs to this one

00:35:15.140 --> 00:35:17.600
central platform for logging.

00:35:17.600 --> 00:35:21.860
And you must write the
good code, but I don't.

00:35:21.860 --> 00:35:24.245
[LAUGHTER] And so, we still
need other tracking in there.

00:35:24.245 --> 00:35:25.620
Because there are
some exceptions

00:35:25.620 --> 00:35:28.410
that only happen on
servers in production.

00:35:28.410 --> 00:35:32.040
So for that, we ended up using
a Stackdriver Error Tracking.

00:35:32.040 --> 00:35:34.290
So any exceptions that we
are not catching-- there

00:35:34.290 --> 00:35:36.270
are a lot of times
that you deploy

00:35:36.270 --> 00:35:38.683
something in the real time,
and you make mistakes.

00:35:38.683 --> 00:35:40.350
So make sure that
there's something that

00:35:40.350 --> 00:35:41.475
can catch those exceptions.

00:35:41.475 --> 00:35:45.670
And rather than failing
abruptly, you fail gracefully.

00:35:45.670 --> 00:35:51.513
MARTIN OMANDER: And you drew
some blue hexagons here.

00:35:51.513 --> 00:35:52.930
Let's just make
sure I understood.

00:35:52.930 --> 00:35:55.870
The first one-- for
monitoring and alerts--

00:35:55.870 --> 00:35:56.917
that was Stackdriver--

00:35:56.917 --> 00:35:58.500
VIKRAM TIWARI:
Stackdriver monitoring.

00:35:58.500 --> 00:35:59.470
MARTIN OMANDER:
Stackdriver monitoring.

00:35:59.470 --> 00:36:01.390
Yeah, and for logging,
it was Stackdriver--

00:36:01.390 --> 00:36:02.470
VIKRAM TIWARI:
Stackdriver Logging.

00:36:02.470 --> 00:36:03.200
It's so easy.

00:36:03.200 --> 00:36:05.182
MARTIN OMANDER:
Oh, I have a guess

00:36:05.182 --> 00:36:06.390
what the last one is called--

00:36:06.390 --> 00:36:09.234
Stackdriver Error Tracking?

00:36:09.234 --> 00:36:10.150
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah.

00:36:10.150 --> 00:36:11.860
MARTIN OMANDER: So the
cool thing with these

00:36:11.860 --> 00:36:14.027
is that they are all
serverless-- you don't have set

00:36:14.027 --> 00:36:15.820
up servers that
manage the monitoring

00:36:15.820 --> 00:36:17.830
and logging and error tracking.

00:36:17.830 --> 00:36:20.350
If you have a Google Cloud
Platform project today,

00:36:20.350 --> 00:36:23.470
you can just click on the
hamburger menu up on the left

00:36:23.470 --> 00:36:28.500
and turn on these
services in your account.

00:36:28.500 --> 00:36:32.792
But you did-- so you
set all of this up.

00:36:32.792 --> 00:36:33.757
This took some time--

00:36:33.757 --> 00:36:34.840
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah, yeah.

00:36:34.840 --> 00:36:35.130
MARTIN OMANDER: --to
migrate everything over.

00:36:35.130 --> 00:36:36.672
VIKRAM TIWARI: So
our whole journey--

00:36:36.672 --> 00:36:38.410
it's not turnkey solution.

00:36:38.410 --> 00:36:41.050
It takes time to rewrite
your code from Python

00:36:41.050 --> 00:36:42.370
to Apache Beam.

00:36:42.370 --> 00:36:44.970
And using a big
Node.js project--

00:36:44.970 --> 00:36:47.860
to one single function-- to
do all these predictions,

00:36:47.860 --> 00:36:48.640
it took some time.

00:36:48.640 --> 00:36:50.620
And adding all these
features in there--

00:36:50.620 --> 00:36:54.100
so this is a multi-year journey
that took almost a year,

00:36:54.100 --> 00:36:56.110
end to end, to build
all these solutions

00:36:56.110 --> 00:36:58.245
and run it totally serverless.

00:36:58.245 --> 00:36:59.620
MARTIN OMANDER:
Yeah, all right--

00:36:59.620 --> 00:37:00.250
very good.

00:37:00.250 --> 00:37:00.640
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah.

00:37:00.640 --> 00:37:02.530
MARTIN OMANDER: If you could
do the last slide, Vikram.

00:37:02.530 --> 00:37:03.010
VIKRAM TIWARI: Let's do it.

00:37:03.010 --> 00:37:04.390
MARTIN OMANDER: Let's do
it, should you or I click?

00:37:04.390 --> 00:37:05.000
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yeah.

00:37:05.000 --> 00:37:05.910
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, you clicked.

00:37:05.910 --> 00:37:06.702
VIKRAM TIWARI: Yes.

00:37:06.702 --> 00:37:08.586
MARTIN OMANDER: Excellent.

00:37:08.586 --> 00:37:12.290
BOTH: So solve business
problems, not engineering

00:37:12.290 --> 00:37:14.680
problems, and go serverless.

